Vulnerability Title

Stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) in Sandbox Model Name Field

Risk Level

Moderate

CVSS v3.1 Score (Intranet Application)

6.1 – Medium

Vector String:
CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:R/S:C/C:L/I:L/A:N

Vulnerability Category

Cross-Site Scripting (Stored XSS)

OWASP Category

OWASP Top 10 2021 – A03: Injection (Cross-Site Scripting)
https://owasp.org/Top10/A03_2021-Injection/

Vulnerability Description

The application provides a Sandbox module that allows users to create new sandbox models by entering a model name. During testing, it was observed that the application does not properly sanitize or validate user input in the model name field.

By inserting the following payload in the model name field:

<img src=x onerror=alert('XSS')>

the application stores the input and renders it directly in the application interface without proper encoding. When the sandbox model is viewed or loaded, the injected payload executes in the browser, resulting in a JavaScript alert popup.

This confirms the presence of a Stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability, where malicious scripts can be permanently stored in the application and executed when users access the affected page.

Impact

Attackers can execute malicious JavaScript in the browsers of other users.

May lead to session hijacking or theft of authentication tokens.

Attackers could perform actions on behalf of other users within the application.

Potential exposure of sensitive data accessible through the application interface.

Could be leveraged for phishing attacks or further client-side exploitation.

Proof of Concept (PoC) Steps

Step 1: Login to the application with valid user credentials.

Step 2: Navigate to the Sandbox module.

Step 3: Click on Create New Sandbox Model.

Step 4: In the Model Name field, enter the following payload:

<img src=x onerror=alert('XSS')>

Step 5: Save the sandbox model.

Step 6: Open or view the created sandbox model.

Step 7: Observe that a JavaScript alert popup appears, confirming the execution of injected script code.

Recommendations

Implement server-side input validation and sanitization for all user-supplied input fields.

Apply output encoding (HTML entity encoding) before rendering user input in the browser.

Use frameworks or libraries that automatically escape untrusted input.

Implement Content Security Policy (CSP) to restrict execution of malicious scripts.

Conduct regular secure code reviews and penetration testing to identify similar injection issues.

